virtio-snd: pcm_device_base, an offset for the ALSA PCM device numbers

The ALSA device number of a virtio-snd PCM is the function node ID of its streams, which QEMU
always sets to 0, so the card's one PCM is hw:0,0. LG's webOS TV firmware opens its speaker
output by a fixed name: the stock /etc/pulse/system.pa loads module-alsa-sink with
device=hw:0,6 (the LG SoC card's PCM 6). With virtio_snd.pcm_device_base=6 on the kernel
cmdline, the PCM is created as device 6 (/dev/snd/pcmC0D6p) and the stock file runs unmodified.

Only the ALSA device number moves. The node ID used to match streams, jacks and channel maps,
and the PCM's name ("VirtIO PCM 0"), are unchanged. The default, 0, keeps mainline behaviour.

--- a/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm.c
+++ b/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm.c
@@ -28,6 +28,10 @@
 module_param(pcm_period_ms_max, uint, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(pcm_period_ms_max, "Maximum PCM period time in milliseconds");
 
+static u32 pcm_device_base;
+module_param(pcm_device_base, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(pcm_device_base, "ALSA device number of the PCM with function node ID 0");
+
 /* Map for converting VirtIO format to ALSA format. */
 static const snd_pcm_format_t g_v2a_format_map[] = {
 	[VIRTIO_SND_PCM_FMT_IMA_ADPCM] = SNDRV_PCM_FORMAT_IMA_ADPCM,
@@ -426,8 +430,9 @@
 		if (!npbs && !ncps)
 			continue;
 
-		rc = snd_pcm_new(snd->card, VIRTIO_SND_CARD_DRIVER, vpcm->nid,
-				 npbs, ncps, &vpcm->pcm);
+		rc = snd_pcm_new(snd->card, VIRTIO_SND_CARD_DRIVER,
+				 pcm_device_base + vpcm->nid, npbs, ncps,
+				 &vpcm->pcm);
 		if (rc) {
 			dev_err(&vdev->dev, "snd_pcm_new[%u] failed: %d\n",
 				vpcm->nid, rc);
